  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures\nfollow Friday into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk \nconditions. \n\nA strong upper trough over the Northeast this afternoon quickly \ndeparts into the Atlantic tonight. A warm front lifts north through \nthe area tonight, then weak surface ridging builds in and over the \nregion through mid week. Dry conditions prevail with varying \nsouthwest to west winds Tuesday becoming southeast on Wednesday. The \nwarm front brings some cloudiness tonight, but that quickly \ndissipates by late Tuesday morning. An approaching upper trough from \nthe OH and TN Valleys brings increasing clouds Wednesday. The main \nthing to focus on is the well above normal temperatures for Tuesday \nand Wednesday as highs reach the upper 60s to around 70F. \n\nWidespread showers remain likely (70-90% chance) Thursday and into \nFriday as a low pressure system and associated cold front move \nthrough our region. Thursday afternoon and evening, some embedded \nthunderstorms are possible (20-30% chance). As this system advects \neastward off the Mid-Atlantic coast, precipitation could linger into \nSaturday, although primarily confined to the Allegheny Mountains.\nBehind Thursday's cold front, temperatures are forecasted to be\n10-15 degrees below early November normals. These cool \ntemperatures in combination with brisk winds and ample cloud \ncover will result in the season's first noticeable cold period.\n\nThe Allegheny mountains will likely (70-80% chance) see their first \naccumulating snow of the season Friday into Saturday. This far \nout, finer scale features and processes are unable to be \nresolved, so considerable uncertainty in snowfall totals exist. \nWith northwesterly winds, the greatest snowfall amounts will lie\non the windward side of the Allegheny mountains.",
